def e(d,disp,prec,eye,place,head,dig,quote,conf,note):
    return dict(date_iso=d,date_display=disp,precision=prec,eye=eye,place=place,headline=head,digest=dig,quote=quote,confidence=conf,source_note=note)
E=[
e("1789-05-04","Monday 4 May 1789","day","morris","Versailles, a window at Mme de Flahaut's","The Estates-General procession: cheers for the king, silence for the queen",
 "Morris watches the deputies process through Versailles from a friend's window. He notes that the crowd cheers Louis but gives the queen nothing, and he files the coldness away as a political fact.","","verified","Journal de Gouverneur Morris (Plon 1901), entry 4 May 1789, Internet Archive PDF"),
e("1789-05-05","Tuesday 5 May 1789","day","morris","Salle des Menus-Plaisirs, Versailles","An American republican moved to tears by a king's speech",
 "At the opening session Morris finds himself weeping at the king's address. He again notes that the queen receives no public acclaim. The pageant still looks like a monarchy reforming itself, not one about to fall.","","verified","Journal de Gouverneur Morris (Plon 1901), entry 5 May 1789"),
e("1789-07-09","Thursday 9 July 1789","day","young","Palais-Royal, Paris","Pamphlets everywhere, and the court says nothing",
 "Young finds the Palais-Royal coffee houses crowded to the doors, with people listening at the windows to orators. Pamphlets for liberty and against clergy and nobles pour out to the provinces. He is astonished that the court neither answers them nor stops them.","","verified","Travels in France, entry 9 July 1789 (excerpt via Alpha History)"),
e("1789-07-12","Sunday 12 July 1789","day","young","on the road in Lorraine, walking a hill","A peasant woman's dues and taxes, on the day Paris rises",
 "Walking his mare up a hill, Young falls in with a poor woman who lists what she owes a lord in wheat, chickens and oats, besides the king's taxes, with seven children to feed. She has heard that 'great people' mean to help the poor. While Paris erupts that same Sunday over Necker's dismissal, his page shows the grievance that lay under it.","","verified","Travels in France, entry 12 July 1789 (Alpha History excerpt)"),
e("1789-07-14","Tuesday 14 July 1789","day","louis","Versailles","The king's log for the day of the Bastille",
 "Louis's journal records no hunt for the day: the entry is a single word. He was not told of the fall of the Bastille until the next morning, when the Duc de La Rochefoucauld-Liancourt reportedly told him it was not a revolt but a revolution. The word measures the distance between Versailles and Paris, not the king's indifference.","Rien","verified","Journal of Louis XVI, 14 July 1789 (Archives nationales); see liminalweb and Wikipedia 'Storming of the Bastille'"),
e("1789-07-14","Tuesday 14 July 1789","day","hardy","rue Saint-Jacques, Paris","A bookseller records alarm bells, arms hunts and the fortress's fall",
 "Hardy logs the day as rumours reach him: crowds searching for arms and powder, the alarm bell, the attack on the Bastille, and the killing of its governor de Launay and of the merchants' provost Flesselles. As always he writes what 'comes to his knowledge', mixing what he saw with what he heard.","","approx","Hardy, Mes loisirs, July 1789 (Bastien et al. edition); contents summarised from secondary literature, not checked against the edition"),
e("1789-07-14","14 July 1789 (letter written soon after)","day","rigby","streets of Paris","Two heads on pikes in a cheering crowd",
 "Rigby is among the crowds when the news of victory spreads. He sees two bloody heads held up on pikes, said to be those of de Launay, the Bastille's governor, and Flesselles, the merchants' provost, and records his revulsion amid the general joy. He had been cheering the people's courage minutes before.","","approx","Dr. Rigby's Letters from France &c. in 1789 (1880), cited in Wikipedia 'Storming of the Bastille'; the date of the letter itself was not checked"),
e("1789-07-16","Thursday 16 July 1789","day","morris","the Bastille, Paris","Touring the cells of a fortress two days fallen",
 "Morris goes to see the Bastille, already a tourist site where the demolition crews have started work, and walks through its cells. He views it with curiosity and keeps his skepticism about the people who took it.","","approx","Preface to Journal de Gouverneur Morris (Plon 1901) mentions his visit to the Bastille cells on 16 July; the diary text itself was not checked"),
e("1789-07-19","Sunday 19 July 1789","day","jefferson","Hôtel de Langeac, Paris","A minister's dispatch: the Bastille, the militia, the king's surrender",
 "Jefferson sends Foreign Secretary John Jay a long, step-by-step account of the week: the troops ringing the city, Necker's dismissal, the arms seized at the Invalides, the taking of the Bastille, and the king's visit to Paris to accept the new order. He writes as a sympathiser who believes the crisis is ending.","","approx","Jefferson to John Jay, 19 July 1789 (Papers of Thomas Jefferson); cited by Wikipedia 'Storming of the Bastille'; the date is from memory and was not checked in this session"),
e("1789-07-20","Monday 20 July 1789","day","young","Strasbourg","Watching a mob gut Strasbourg's town hall",
 "Young is passing through Strasbourg when a crowd storms and loots the town hall. They throw papers and furniture from the windows while the troops look on. He records how the revolution of Paris reached a provincial city as a riot over bread and municipal grievances.","","verified","Archives d'Alsace, 'fiche pillage 20 juillet 1789' (notes Young's presence that day); Travels in France"),
e("1789-07-22","Wednesday 22 July 1789","day","morris","Palais-Royal arcade, Paris","Foulon's head arrives in triumph while Morris waits for his carriage",
 "After dinner Morris is strolling under the Palais-Royal arcade when a crowd brings in the head of the old minister Foulon on a pike, with the naked body dragged behind. He then learns that the remains were shown to Foulon's son-in-law, the intendant Bertier, who was killed and cut to pieces in turn. He notes Foulon's 'crime': accepting a place in the ministry. The entry ends with a cry of disgust at the cruelty of the people.","","approx","Diary, 22 July 1789 (Diary and Letters, 1888). Not read directly here: the passage under the Palais-Royal arcade, waiting for a carriage, appears in English at revolution.chnm.org/d/384, where it is credited to the Duchesse de Gontaut and dated 14 July. Its wording and details fit Morris's diary for 22 July."),
e("1789-07-22","Wednesday 22 July 1789","day","hardy","Paris, Place de Grève reports","The lantern, the hay in the mouth, the son-in-law",
 "Hardy records the arrest of Foulon, who had been rumoured to say the hungry could eat grass, his hanging from the lamp-post at the Grève when the rope broke, and his head paraded with hay in its mouth. Bertier was brought in from Compiègne the same evening and killed. The bookseller's horror is mixed with a sense that the crowd felt it was doing justice.","","approx","Hardy, Mes loisirs, 22 July 1789; summarised from secondary literature"),
e("1789-07-22","Wednesday 22 July 1789 (with postscript)","day","jefferson","Paris","Telling Madison the whole revolution in one letter",
 "Jefferson tells Madison the story from the royal session of 23 June, through the troop build-up and the fall of the Bastille, to the king's return of Necker. In a postscript he adds that Foulon has been seized and killed by the mob and that Bertier is reported massacred. He presents the killings as an afterword to a victory.","","verified","Jefferson to Madison, 22 July 1789, Founders Online"),
e("1789-07-23","Thursday 23 July 1789","day","jefferson","Paris","Foulon and Bertier, then a note about money",
 "Writing to Thomas Paine, Jefferson describes Foulon dragged through the main streets headless and Bertier killed on arrival. He hopes the violence will stop, since only the capture of other hated men could restart it. He then turns to Paine's money troubles and mentions that Gouverneur Morris leaves for London in a few days.","","verified","Jefferson to Paine, 23 July 1789, Founders Online"),
e("1789-07-25","late July 1789","range","bombelles","French embassy, Venice","The news of Paris reaches a royalist ambassador",
 "In Venice, Bombelles receives by courier the news of the Bastille, the king's capitulation in Paris and the first emigrations. He takes it as a catastrophe for throne and altar and as a personal grief for the royal family his wife serves.","","approx","Journal du marquis de Bombelles (Droz edition), July 1789; exact entry not checked"),
e("1789-07-28","c. 24–31 July 1789","range","young","Franche-Comté (Besançon region)","Châteaux burning, and an Englishman made to wear a cockade",
 "Riding south, Young passes through country where peasants have attacked and burned châteaux and seized the lords' papers (the Great Fear). Villagers stop him for not wearing the tricolour cockade, and he has to talk his way past suspicion that he is an agent of the aristocrats. The traveller has become a suspect.","","approx","Travels in France, late July 1789 entries; exact day not checked"),
e("1789-10-05","Monday 5 October 1789","day","louis","Châtillon woods near Meudon, then Versailles","Shooting interrupted: the women reach Versailles",
 "Louis is out shooting at the Porte de Châtillon and logs his bag as usual, 81 head of game. He notes that the day was interrupted by 'events' and that he rode out and back. Those events were thousands of Parisian women, then Lafayette's National Guard, marching on Versailles over bread.","","approx","Journal of Louis XVI, 5 October 1789 (Nicolardot edition); widely cited, but not checked in this session"),
e("1789-10-05","Monday 5 October 1789","day","hardy","Paris","The market women march out in the rain",
 "Hardy records the women of the Halles and the faubourgs gathering at the Hôtel de Ville over the price and shortage of bread, dragging cannon and marching off toward Versailles. The National Guard follows them by evening. He notes the rumours of an insult to the cockade at a royal banquet that had inflamed the city.","","approx","Hardy, Mes loisirs, 5 October 1789; summarised from secondary literature"),
e("1789-10-06","Tuesday 6 October 1789","day","louis","Versailles to Paris","The king's terse log of leaving Versailles forever",
 "The journal notes a departure for Paris after noon, a visit to the Hôtel de Ville, then supper and bed at the Tuileries. It gives no hint of the dawn invasion of the palace, the murdered bodyguards, or the crowd escorting the 'baker, baker's wife and baker's boy'. It is the same flat style as a hunting day.","","approx","Journal of Louis XVI, 6 October 1789; widely cited, not checked in this session"),
e("1789-10-06","Tuesday 6 October 1789","day","hardy","Paris","Heads on pikes ahead of the royal carriage",
 "Hardy hears that the crowd broke into the palace at dawn, and he records the long procession that evening bringing the royal family into Paris, with bread wagons and the heads of slain guards carried ahead. Within weeks his 36-year journal stops, as if the city he chronicled had ended.","","approx","Hardy, Mes loisirs, 6 October 1789; journal ends October 1789 (Wikipedia)"),
e("1790-07-14","Wednesday 14 July 1790","day","williams","Champ de Mars, Paris","The Federation: a revolution as a festival",
 "Williams, newly arrived from London, watches the Festival of the Federation: a vast earthwork amphitheatre that Parisians of every class had dug by hand, the oath at the altar of the nation, and rain that did not dampen the crowd. She writes that she has seen the triumph of human liberty and that her heart was won.","","approx","Letters Written in France (1790), letter on the Federation; dates of her letters are loose"),
e("1791-02-15","February 1791","month","elisabeth","the Tuileries, Paris","Why she will not follow her aunts abroad",
 "As her elderly aunts leave for Rome, Élisabeth explains in a letter that she does not believe her duty calls her to emigrate. She will stay beside her brother, a decision that will take her to the Temple and the scaffold.","","approx","Letter of February 1791 (cited in Wikipedia, Élisabeth of France)"),
e("1791-06-20","night of 20–21 June 1791","day","marie-therese","the Tuileries, Paris","Woken at night, dressed as someone else",
 "Marie-Thérèse later remembered being woken and disguised. Her small brother was dressed as a girl, and the governess Madame de Tourzel posed as a Russian baroness, while the family slipped out of the Tuileries around midnight. The child understood only that they were going somewhere secret.","","verified","Her account of the flight to Varennes (later memoir; Alpha History); the departure time is confirmed by Wikipedia 'Flight to Varennes'"),
e("1791-06-21","Tuesday 21 June 1791","day","louis","Varennes-en-Argonne","A journey logged like a hunt: left at midnight, arrested at eleven",
 "The king's journal records the flight in two clauses: departure from Paris at midnight, and arrival and arrest at Varennes at eleven in the evening. Postmaster Drouet had recognised him at Sainte-Menehould. The disaster that destroyed his credibility reads like an itinerary.","","approx","Journal of Louis XVI, 21 June 1791; widely cited, not checked in this session"),
e("1791-07-15","summer 1791","range","elisabeth","the Tuileries, after the return","The absurd return with Barnave and Pétion",
 "Writing to Angélique de Bombelles, Élisabeth describes the slow, humiliating return from Varennes squeezed in the coach with the Assembly's deputies Barnave and Pétion. With dark humour she calls the scene ridiculous. Barnave's courtesy to the queen on that ride would later help convict him.","","approx","Letter to Mme de Bombelles on the Varennes journey (Wikipedia, Élisabeth of France); exact date not checked"),
e("1791-07-14","Thursday 14 July 1791","day","morris","Paris","An anecdote of the king's childhood cruelty",
 "On the second anniversary of the Bastille, three weeks after Varennes, Morris hears a story of Louis's cruelty to animals as a boy. He writes in disgust that it is no wonder such a creature is being dethroned. The admirer of 1789 has turned contemptuous.","","verified","Journal de Gouverneur Morris (Plon 1901), preface citing the entry of 14 July 1791"),
e("1792-06-10","Sunday 10 June 1792","day","morris","Paris (now US Minister)","France like cattle before a storm",
 "A week after presenting his credentials to Louis XVI, Morris reports to Jefferson: war with Austria going badly, an undisciplined army, paper money out of control, the cabinet split. He warns that a nation this exhausted may accept despotism for the sake of quiet.","Cattle before a Thunder Storm.","verified","Morris to Jefferson, 10 June 1792, Founders Online (Jefferson/01-24-02-0049)"),
e("1792-08-10","Friday 10 August 1792","day","jullien","Paris","A republican mother hears the cannon at the Tuileries",
 "Rosalie Jullien writes to her husband as the faubourgs and the fédérés storm the Tuileries and the Swiss Guards fall. She describes the gunfire, the tocsin and the bodies, and she rejoices that the 'tyrant' has been overthrown. Her enthusiasm is the counterpoint to the royal family's terror that same morning.","","approx","Lockroy (ed.), Journal d'une bourgeoise pendant la Révolution (1881); L. Parker, Writing the Revolution (2013); exact letter date not checked"),
e("1792-08-10","Friday 10 August 1792","day","marie-therese","the Tuileries, then the Assembly","Leaving the palace for the last time",
 "Marie-Thérèse remembered the family leaving the Tuileries early in the morning, crossing the garden to take refuge with the Legislative Assembly, and spending hours crammed into the cramped reporters' box while fighting raged at the palace behind them. The monarchy was suspended before evening.","","verified","Her later memoir; the event and date are confirmed by Wikipedia 'Insurrection of 10 August 1792'"),
e("1792-08-13","Monday 13 August 1792","day","clery","the Temple, Paris","The family enters the Temple",
 "Cléry records the royal family's transfer to the Temple, first to the small tower and then to the great tower, where he would serve the king. He describes the bare rooms, the guards' insults, and the family's attempt to keep a daily routine of lessons and meals.","","approx","Cléry, Journal (London 1798); the Temple date is confirmed by Wikipedia 'Marie-Thérèse of France'; when Cléry himself arrived was not checked"),
e("1792-08-17","Friday 17 August 1792","day","morris","Paris","Business as usual, a week after the monarchy fell",
 "A week after the Tuileries massacre, Morris's official letter to Jefferson deals with foreign officers' pay certificates, Kosciuszko's claim on the United States, and a squabble over the vice-consulship at Dunkirk. The paperwork of a small republic carries on through the collapse of a great monarchy.","","verified","Morris to Jefferson, 17 August 1792, Founders Online (Jefferson/01-24-02-0289)"),
e("1792-08-30","Thursday 30 August 1792","day","morris","Paris","His house searched; Saint-Domingue's debt; staying on",
 "Morris refuses to sign a contract with the new ministers for money to relieve Saint-Domingue, which is burning in a slave revolt, saying he has no authority to deal with them. Section commissioners search his house for hidden arms on an informer's word, and he sends them off. The British ambassador has gone, but Morris stays.","","verified","Morris to Jefferson, 30 August 1792, Founders Online (Jefferson/01-24-02-0312)"),
e("1792-09-03","night of 3 September 1792","day","restif","outside La Force prison, Paris","A night-walker at the prison gates",
 "Restif describes wandering through the September nights as the prisons were emptied by summary 'tribunals' and the prisoners butchered. He claims to have seen the Princesse de Lamballe, the queen's friend, dragged out and killed, and her body mutilated. He writes as a horrified witness, in prose that is clearly literary.","","approx","Les Nuits de Paris (Alpha History excerpt, 'Retif describes the September Massacres'); Lamballe was killed on 3 September; Restif's genre is flagged"),
e("1792-09-10","Monday 10 September 1792","day","morris","Paris","An ambassador's tally of the September killings",
 "Morris reports that the killing began on the afternoon of the 2nd and went on about a week, with perhaps thousands dead. Among them were hundreds of non-juring priests, the Abbaye prisoners, Madame de Lamballe, the Duc de La Rochefoucauld murdered under escort, and his old acquaintance Montmorin. He notes that there is no real executive left to stop it.","","verified","Morris to Jefferson, 10 September 1792, Founders Online (Jefferson/01-24-02-0335)"),
e("1792-12-26","Wednesday 26 December 1792","day","wollstonecraft","her lodgings in the Marais, Paris","The king rides past to his trial",
 "From her window Wollstonecraft watches Louis driven to the Convention to hear his defence. The streets are silent and lined with National Guards. She is surprised to find herself in tears at his dignity, and alone that night she is frightened by her own imagination.","","approx","Letter to Joseph Johnson, 26 December 1792, Posthumous Works (1798); not re-checked in this session"),
e("1793-01-10","Thursday 10 January 1793","day","morris","Paris","The king's fate: he must die or reign",
 "Writing privately to Washington, Morris passes on the king's own reckoning: if the Convention refers his case to the people, he expects a mob to kill him. Morris's view is blunter. Five days earlier his diary had fallen silent, and it would stay silent until October 1794.","he must die or reign","verified","Morris to Washington, 10 January 1793, Founders Online; the diary gap (5 Jan 1793–12 Oct 1794) is from the LOC finding aid"),
e("1793-01-20","Sunday 20 January 1793, evening","day","clery","the Temple","The last meeting with the family",
 "Cléry describes the evening the king was allowed to see his wife, sister and children for the last time, in the dining room behind a glass door that the guards watched. He tells of the weeping, the promise to see them in the morning, and the king's decision to spare them a second farewell.","","approx","Cléry, Journal (1798); memoir-like text"),
e("1793-01-20","Sunday 20 January 1793, evening","day","marie-therese","the Temple","A daughter's last evening with her father",
 "Marie-Thérèse remembered her father's calm and the family's grief, and that he told the children to forgive his killers. She remembered fainting at the end, and that he did not come in the morning as promised.","","approx","Memoir of the captivity in the Temple (written about 1795)"),
e("1793-01-21","Monday 21 January 1793, 5 a.m.","day","clery","the Temple","Waking the king for the last time",
 "Cléry wakes Louis at five, dresses him, and serves the Mass that Edgeworth says in the room. The king hands him small keepsakes for the queen and the children. Cléry is not allowed to go with him, and he hears the drums carry the carriage away.","","verified","Cléry, Journal (1798); the details are confirmed by Wikipedia 'Execution of Louis XVI'"),
e("1793-01-21","Monday 21 January 1793, c. 10:22 a.m.","day","edgeworth","Place de la Révolution","On the scaffold steps",
 "Edgeworth rides with the king to the Place de la Révolution and persuades him to let his hands be tied. He climbs the scaffold beside him and sees Louis try to speak to the crowd before Santerre's drums drown him out. He kneels as the blade falls. The famous blessing attributed to him is a tradition he never confirmed.","","verified","Edgeworth, Memoirs (1815); the time and details are from Wikipedia 'Execution of Louis XVI'"),
e("1793-01-22","late January 1793","range","ruault","Paris","A bookseller's quiet dread after the execution",
 "Ruault writes to his brother about the execution and how the city took it: shops shut, the streets watched, some people dipping cloths in the blood, most people silent. He is a patriot, but he is no longer sure where the Revolution is going.","","approx","Gazette d'un Parisien sous la Révolution (1976); the date and content of this letter were not checked"),
e("1793-02-15","Friday 15 February 1793","day","wollstonecraft","Paris","A letter on the 'present character' of the French",
 "In an open letter to Johnson, Wollstonecraft sets out to judge the French nation and admits that her hopes are under strain. She sees vanity, theatricality and bloodshed where she expected virtue, but she still insists that the old regime made them so. It is a radical's first doubts, written three weeks after the king's death.","","approx","'Letter on the Present Character of the French Nation', dated 15 February 1793, Posthumous Works (1798)"),
e("1793-10-12","October 1793","month","williams","the Luxembourg prison, Paris","An English admirer of the Revolution arrested",
 "When the Convention orders the arrest of British subjects, Williams and her mother and sister are taken into custody and spend weeks in prison. She later wrote of fellow prisoners, Girondins and their friends among them, going one by one to the tribunal. The enthusiast of 1790 now sees the Revolution from inside its prisons.","","approx","Letters from France (later series); the month is approximate"),
e("1793-10-16","Wednesday 16 October 1793, before dawn","day","marie-antoinette","the Conciergerie","The queen's last letter, to Élisabeth",
 "Hours after her conviction, the queen writes to her sister-in-law that she is innocent, at peace with God, and anxious for her children, and she asks Élisabeth to tell her son never to avenge them. She was guillotined at about a quarter past noon. The letter never reached Élisabeth, who was executed in May 1794.","","verified","Wikipedia 'Marie Antoinette' (last letter and execution time)"),
e("1794-12-30","Tuesday 30 December 1794","day","morris","Hamburg (after leaving Paris on 12 October 1794)","Looking back on the Terror from Hamburg",
 "Finally out of France, Morris tells Washington what he could not write down: daily misery he could do nothing about, the humiliation of dealing with 'the worst of mankind', and a woman arrested in his own house by the Committee of General Safety. He predicts the Revolution will end in a single despotism. His diary resumed only after he left Paris.","","verified","Morris to Washington, 30 December 1794, Founders Online (Washington/05-17-02-0231)"),
]
conv=[
 {"date":"1789-07-14","label":"Fall of the Bastille","eyes":["louis","hardy","rigby"],"note":"The king's log has nothing; the bookseller lists rumours; the English doctor sees the heads go by. The same day reads as an empty page, a chronicle and a trauma."},
 {"date":"1789-07-22","label":"Lynching of Foulon and Bertier","eyes":["morris","hardy","jefferson"],"note":"Morris, a few yards away, is horrified; Hardy reports it as the people's justice gone wild; Jefferson, writing the same night, treats it as a postscript to a victory. It shows how distance and politics shape a description."},
 {"date":"1789-10-05","label":"The women's march on Versailles","eyes":["louis","hardy"],"note":"Louis logs 81 head of game and an 'interruption'; Hardy logs the women leaving Paris with cannon. Two pages written the same day show the gap that the march itself closed."},
 {"date":"1791-06-21","label":"Flight to Varennes","eyes":["louis","marie-therese","elisabeth"],"note":"Three members of one family in one coach: the king's itinerary, the child's memory of disguises, and the sister's sardonic letter about the return."},
 {"date":"1792-08-10","label":"Fall of the monarchy","eyes":["jullien","marie-therese","morris"],"note":"The republican mother cheers, the princess hides in the reporters' box, and a week later the American minister files routine paperwork."},
 {"date":"1793-01-21","label":"Execution of Louis XVI","eyes":["clery","edgeworth","marie-therese","ruault","morris"],"note":"The servant in the tower, the priest on the scaffold, the daughter left behind and the bookseller in the street, while the best-placed foreign diarist in Paris writes nothing: Morris's diary is silent from 5 January."},
]
ties=[
 {"a":"morris","b":"jefferson","kind":"met","note":"Close friends and fellow Americans in Paris. They had silhouettes made together at the Palais-Royal on 22 April 1789, and Jefferson mentions Morris in his letter of 23 July."},
 {"a":"morris","b":"louis","kind":"met","note":"Morris was presented to Louis XVI as US Minister on 3 June 1792 and later helped plan an escape attempt for him."},
 {"a":"elisabeth","b":"bombelles","kind":"mentioned","note":"Élisabeth's chief correspondent was Angélique de Bombelles, the ambassador's wife, so letters from the Tuileries fed his journal."},
 {"a":"marie-antoinette","b":"elisabeth","kind":"relative","note":"The queen's last letter was addressed to her sister-in-law, who never received it."},
 {"a":"louis","b":"marie-therese","kind":"relative","note":"Father and daughter, writing about the same coach at Varennes and the same farewell in the Temple."},
 {"a":"clery","b":"edgeworth","kind":"met","note":"Both attended the king on the morning of 21 January 1793. Cléry served the Mass that Edgeworth said."},
 {"a":"williams","b":"wollstonecraft","kind":"met","note":"Both were in the British radical circle in Paris in 1793, and Wollstonecraft visited Williams's salon."},
 {"a":"hardy","b":"ruault","kind":"same street","note":"Both were Paris booksellers of the Latin Quarter trade; Ruault's letters continue where Hardy's journal stops."},
 {"a":"jullien","b":"marie-therese","kind":"opposite sides","note":"On 10 August 1792 one cheers the overthrow while the other hides from it in the Assembly."},
]
threads=[
 {"to":"russia-1812","note":"The Revolution's armies became Napoleon's. The émigré nobles of 1789 to 1792 turned up in Russian service, and the Grande Armée that entered Moscow descended from the levée of 1793."},
 {"to":"us-civil-war","note":"Jefferson, the Paris witness of 1789, wrote the Declaration whose promises the Civil War fought over. Morris's 1792 letters on aid to Saint-Domingue touch the Haitian Revolution, which frightened American slaveholders for decades."},
 {"to":"russia-1917","note":"The Bolsheviks read their revolution through the French one: Jacobins, Terror, and fear of a 'Thermidor' shaped Lenin's and Trotsky's debates."},
 {"to":"long-threads","note":"The guillotine, the metric system, the citizen army and the very words 'left' and 'right' run from 1789 through every later chapter."},
]
images=[
 {"url":"https://www.revolution.chnm.org/d/5","title":"The day of 21 July 1789: scaling and pillaging of the Hôtel de Ville in Strasbourg","caption":"A colour drawing of the Strasbourg town-hall riot that Arthur Young watched; the archives date the pillage to 20 July, the drawing's title to the 21st.","date":"1789","credit":"Johann Hans; BnF, Collection Hennin (via Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, RRCHNM)"},
 {"url":"https://collections.library.yale.edu/catalog/10968001","title":"Le palais royal de Paris: a peep at the French monstrosities","caption":"A British satirical print of the Palais-Royal, where Young heard the orators and Morris saw Foulon's head arrive.","date":"c. 1790s","credit":"Yale University Library"},
 {"url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Execution%20of%20Louis%20XVI","title":"Execution of Louis XVI (Wikimedia Commons category)","caption":"Period engravings of 21 January 1793, the day Cléry, Edgeworth and Ruault describe.","date":"1793","credit":"Various; Wikimedia Commons"},
 {"url":"https://omeka.cloud.unimelb.edu.au/execution-ballads/items/show/1133","title":"The Execution of Louis XVI, 21st January 1793 (broadside ballad)","caption":"A popular broadside showing how the king's death was sold to readers abroad.","date":"1793","credit":"University of Melbourne, Execution Ballads collection"},
 {"url":"https://digitalcollections.udel.edu/Documents/Detail/louis-xvi.-recit-de-ce-qui-sest-passe-a-la-seance-tenue-par-le-roi-le-5-juillet.-montauban-de-limprimerie-de-vincent-teulieres-seul-imprimeur-du-roi-1789./326476","title":"Récit de ce qui s'est passé à la séance tenue par le roi (provincial print, 1789)","caption":"A royal session reported in print for the provinces: how news of Versailles reached readers like Young's innkeepers.","date":"1789","credit":"University of Delaware Library"},
]
